# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Line endings.
#
# Without this file the only thing deciding line endings was each developer's
# `core.autocrlf`, which on Windows is `true`: every checkout rewrote LF to
# CRLF and every commit rewrote it back, announcing itself with a
# "LF will be replaced by CRLF the next time Git touches it" warning on each
# file. Tools that write files directly (scripts, formatters, generators) do
# not take part in that dance, so the working tree drifted into a mix - and
# some files ended up with both endings inside the one file.
#
# LF is canonical here, in the repository and in the working tree on every
# platform, which is what Node, Next.js, ESLint and Prettier all assume.
# `eol=lf` takes precedence over `core.autocrlf`, so no one has to change
# their local Git config for this to hold.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary formats: never eligible for end-of-line conversion. `text=auto` would
# detect most of these anyway, but xlsx in particular is a zip archive that
# corrupts silently if anything rewrites bytes inside it.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.ico  binary
*.xlsx binary
*.pdf  binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf  binary

# Lockfiles are generated: keep them out of diffs and merge conflicts.
package-lock.json -diff linguist-generated
